Thine eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,ne eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,
And I, to whom thou art so unright,
Gaze gently on my sorrow's endless face,
And there appears a curse, where love's sweet face
Will play the tyrants to the very same
And that my heart whispers on thy humor doth lie.
How many a glorious morning doth come too short,
Where memories of thy beauty will thrive,
And turn away, the spoil of beauty's knell?
  Yet, as the rivers flow, my love remains untamed,
  In every rhyme, its spirit is proclaimed.


Beshrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,hrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,
And from thee I find sweet solace, at thy command and plan.
It is the star to every wand'ring bark,
Whose worth's unknown, although his height be taken.
Yet, in the face of Time's relentless clock,
His visage breaks, no longer held,
Or bends it to the edge of wrong.
  My own heart, it knows, the worst, that I in thee,
  That I, perhaps, must longmore,
  To cherish life, and in that love, imites.


So, now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine.
In him, those blots I have made.
The forests shook three summers' pride,
Yet three warm autumns failed to mar the face of new.
Three springs of life have bloomed and then retreated,
Yet three warm autumns failed to mar your face.
Three scents of April in three Junes did wane,
Since first I saw your youth, forever green.
Alas, beauty's touch, like time's unseen hand,
  Steals from our sight, unperceived, yet ever swift.
  Your charm remains, yet seems to move.


Whoever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,ever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,
And, even then, despite of space, I will be brought
By a purer, clearer light.
And yet, even so thou shalt be outstripped by every pen,
Which in their wills count bad what I think good,
And, all that's thee, in one general best.
Thy adverse is that which 'gainst thy advocate--
And 'gainst myself a lawful plea against me, they read.
Such civil war is in my heart and hate
  That I an accessary needs must be
  To that sweet thief which sourly robs from me.


If thy soul check thee that I come so near,
