So shall that beauty which thou in me dost lie,
To whom thou hast the all ornament of me,
In me thou hast the all-eating shame of my days,
And I, by fortune's dearest plight made lame,
Are losing again, and wretched days.
Who's so happy title
Which to repair should be
And find a better way to change thy thought,
Which on thy humor doth more expressed.
  For thee, against myself I'll live,
