Thine eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,ne eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,
And as long as heart and eye doth stand,
Gaze once again on these bower.
The roses have thorns, and crystal streams, mud;
Clouds mar the sky, and the sea,
Existings the siren song, as the doth stand.
In witness to thine eyes, and my heart,
They glimpse the beauty of my thought,
And judge it by actions wrought,
Yet, like thine eyes, I think it nigh,
The worst, which this slander born of deeds far from clear.
