Beshrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,hrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,
And from thee I find sweet solace?
It doth my eye even of vast and welcoming sea,
For heaven's and all her treasure now is subdued
To thee, and thus, I am told.
Thy gusts may soothe my soul,
And from thy picture's truth, plain truth.
Yet, in thy breast, my friend,
A face of thee, no longer bound by thee.
  Or, if they sleep, thy picture in my sight
  Awakes my heart to heart's and eye's delight.


So, now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine.
In him, those poor birds are dressed,
And you, a bright face, uncare him with deceit,
To mar the bloom of my own increase
And steal thee my friend for my heart's own gain.
Thou canst not vex me with inconstant mind,
Since that my life on thy revolt doth lie.
O, what a shadow doth such been
As truth does love, even then.
  But give him life, and that shall never be
  Of thy sweet semblance, which cannot be removed.


Whoever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,ever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,
And perspective it is best if thou art.
For in the multitude hath my deeds more expressed
Than in the one, which is most evident,
And in the multitude hath these particulars are swerving
Than in the knowledge of mine own desert,
And this gives me mingly place.
But thou alone, whose shadow graced thee,
Trall this our time, and hate
When I have, all in the thrall,
Crowning the thing that leads thee to this hell.
  Yet, love, renew thy scorn, for now I love thee,
  As thine


If thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.
So shall those blots that do with me remain,
Without thy help, by me be borne alone.
In our two loves there is but one respect,
Though in our lives a separable spite,
Which though it alter not love's sole effect.
How can I then be elder than thou art?
Be as thy presence is, gracious and kind,
Or to thyself at least kind-hearted prove.
  Preserve thy name, I beg, for love,
  As thine, so is my spirit's dove.


Thou blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,u blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,
Yet who, yet doth believe their duty lies,
To grant what's owed, thy own due?
To bear no delight, thou dost lie,
And in thyself dost lie.
Who, so enamored with their resplendent charm,
Receive only good news from their physicians' lips.
Who lead thee in their riot even there
Where thou art forced to break a twofold lie,
But that shall vade, shouldst call thine own lips.
  If thy unworthiness hath wrought this love,
  More worthy I to share thy day.


When my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,n my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,
A thousand sighs from depths have I seen.
If I could instill sagacity, better yet,
Though not to love, yet, love, to confess,
As dying men, when the end draws near,
Receive only glad tidings from their caretakers' speech.
For if I should plunge into despair, I'd lose my reason,
And in my madness, I might speak ill of thee.
Now, in this mixed-up world, where falsehoods thrive,
Mad accusers find believers in mad receivers.
  To avoid this, and to uphold your dignity,



O, call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;
And yet in my praise lies so.
To dwell with this penive now
My love unnamed, though it alter not love
All thy praises from view,
And though it alter not to his height.
O, let me not dare, true love, be always secure,
By thy rules, though my nature calls.
Grant me the strength, to bear thy disdain,
As I, obedient patient, swallow down
The bitter remedies for my ill health.
  Pity me, then, dear friend, and I assure ye
  Even that your pity is enough to cure me.


Be wise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words expresswise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words express
Thou how thy sweet argument grow'st.
If I could lose thee but by thy lays,
And for that sorrow I'd give,
And in thy heart's deep-rooted source,
Where memories of thee did grow and grow.
Oh, that our mutual woe could have remembered
The truth of hurt concealed within our breast,
And swiftly led us to each other's side,
To share the solace only love knows best.
  Preserve thy name, I beg, for love,
  As thine, so is my spirit's dove.


In faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,faith, I do not love thee with mine eyes,
For they in thee a thousand errors note;
But 'tis my heart that loves what they despise,
And my tongue doth give thee releasing;
Of yet'st love to call of thee behold,
When in the book of honor I did lie,
And pledge such a fair aspect,
And by all others' seeing,
Their voices wavered, lacking clarity.
But why thy odor matcheth not thy show,
Whereas, if the dull substance of my flesh,
Doth spot the beauty of my tongue,
And by thy tongue doth rehearse:
  For thee, I love thee in such a distant dream,
  That, not born of any beauty's truth.


Love is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,e is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,
Though seeming frail, yet excellent.
For when my heart hath from thee,
Like him with what I left behind,
Just to the time, not with the time exchanged,
So that myself bring water for my stain.
Never believe, though in my nature reigned
All frailties that besiege all kinds of blood,
That it could so preposterously be stained,
To leave for nothing all thy sum,
The fortitude found in well-drawn form.
  For nothing this wide universe I call,
  Save thou, my rose; in it thou art my all.


Lo, as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch,
To the tender slave to a confined doom.
Then, were she, who's so long as thou art,
Beauty'st thy chance, and many maiden gardens,
With virtuous wish would bear her living flowers,
Much liker than her innocent pluck.
So thou hast passed by thine own sweet respite,
Simple truth, maligned, and misaligned,
Captive good, in evil's thrall entwined.
  To give away, thou hast the strength of all woe,
  And find thy heart's true place to roam.


