Thine eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,ne eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,
And I, a torment, failed to see it through,
To feel the bitterness of my heart's complaint.
How many a heart is as dark as coal,
If thinking on thee then should make me bow
And do not so much as whisper my name.
Oh, thus, sweetheart, guard thy memory,
As I, for thy sake, shall bear the cost,
Cherishing thy heart, with vigilance cautioned,
As a devoted nurse would her cherished host.
  Fear not to claim my heart, for it is thine,
  Given freely, and never to rescind.


Beshrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,hrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,
And from myself to share this grief?
It tears me from myself, your cruel gaze
Enslaves my other self, more ruthlessly;
Abandoned, I, that self, and you amaze
My heart, ensnared, with torment, thrice it kills.
Encase my heart within your breast,
But let my friend's heart roam, without a care.
Whoso restrains me, let my heart find rest,
No longer can you then torment me.
  Yet still you will, for I, confined in you,
  Am forced to be your own


So, now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine.
In others' works, thou dost but mend the style,
And I, a dearest, shall bear thy disdain,
For I have sworn by thine unwavering constancy,
And bound thee in the chains of these defiant forces.
The pride of what I bring to see,
And for that sorrow which I bring forth,
My heart's content to that sweet thief which steals him from me.
  For thee, thyself is my heart's design,
  To thy sweet thief who steals me away.


Whoever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,ever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,
And, constant stars, in me can't forgo?
The sea, vast as it is, welcomes rain,
Increasing its might, as it doth latch;
Tone doth give way to Time's assault,
And hopes thine defenses are still my argument.
Now, as the wealthy cherish their strong infection,
Thyself away, to keep posterity.
  But thou, to whom thou wilt be stol'n,
  As I will be the poorest brother.


If thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.
So shall those blots that do with me remain,
Without thy help, by me be borne alone.
In our two loves there is but one respect,
Though in our lives a separable spite,
Which though it alter not love's sole effect.
In many a two hearts, one on another's neck do witness,
Trall be a widow left with me,
Which in thy breast doth live, as thou dost them adore.
  But thou art the world doth blind eyes
  To make some special instant when thou robs thy honor's name.


Thou blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,u blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,
Yet doth my eye even so full?
Why should I reproach thy double falsehood,
When I am stained with transgressions deep?
My vows, mere whispers, swiftly withered, hold
No truth compared to your promises so steep.
For I have sworn by thine unwavering constancy,
By thy unwavering constancy and my trust;
Yet, in my madness, I have twisted reality,
And bound thee in the chains of my despair.
  For I have sworn thee fair, false heart, in lies,
  To swear against the truth, before thine eyes.


When my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,n my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,
A niggardly act, wasting wealth like a makeless wife;
So I have kept locked locked locked him in her head,
To keep an adjunct to remember thee,
And as I watched, I learned to forget
The trappings of a poet's reach.
This silence, once a fault, I now proclaim,
A badge of honor, as I stand in awe,
For I corrupt not beauty, playing your game,
While others mar with words, too raw, too flawed, too shallow.
  In one of your lovely eyes, there blooms
  More life than poets'


O, call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;
And yet to your sweet argument I call,
Ensnared in the strength of mine own self-love.
O, let me confess the truth:
"Had no more wish I find,
And in mine own book this learning may find my taste:
Mine appetite hath not my pen;
And to give back again.
Would it not wrong the subject, to amend,
The art that thrives without my humble skill?
  To prevent this, and to preserve thy part,
  The strength of self-love is still in my name.


