Love is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,e is my sin, and thy dear virtue hate,
Hate of my sin, grounded on sinful loving.
O, but with mine compare thou thine own state,
Though I should scorn, knowing thy heart doth lie.
For what do I not welcome openly,
Although I swear by him, and join her trance,
Thy adverse party is thy advocate--
And 'gainst myself a lawful plea commence.
Such civil strife in my breast,
Is it not vengeance enough to torture me,
And find no reason to incite desire,
The faithfulness and virtue of thy love.
  To part from me, thou hast the power of decree,
  I can but accede, for I see the truth in love.


Lo, as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch as a careful huswife runs to catch
One of her feathered creatures broke away,
Sets down her babe, and makes all swift dispatch,
To the tender inward of well-refined pen.
Yet, as the ocean takes on the tide;
She binds me close, and give me mirth,
A fragrant muse, in vast and deep,
I play along, her falsehoods I endure.
And in this act, I find my heart's tale:
My love, you run near,
My heart, my griefs intensify,
A union of love in time and space.
  Within this poem, your image lives,
  And through them, you'll find your resting place.


