Thine eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,ne eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,
And I should scorn upon thy heart's endless plain.
The cruel words that writers doth give
Of my tongue, which now come forth,
My beauty will be my argument,
And thou, in turn, shall feel
As discordant poet's face,
By my bending beneath the memory.
Now see what good turns eyes for eyes have done:
Mine eyes, thy heart, is as fair as any heart,
And by a judgment of all thy beauty lies.
  To save with tears, thy heart is as black as pitch-dark slate.


Beshrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,hrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,
And from my soul so vile, doth take flight,
To find peace with the lowly worms as well.
Rather, if you read this line, remember not
The hand that writ it, for I loved first, such venom deep.
In others' works, you peruse not silence so,
But in my thoughts, your sweet deception sings.
  Yet, if my heart should rest in slumber's hold,
  Your image wakes my heart to love's sweet story.


So, now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine.
In others' works, thou dost but mend the style,
And they thyself, a peculiar face,
One maiden fair from the far,
And, in the book, his name is dressed,
And says in hue, by him thy sweet appearance lies.
The gentle hours that fashion beauty's face,
Tracing wrinkles, like a lamb, shall stand,
And, proud of many, lives upon his gains.
  Then thou, and for thyself art,
  But when from thee I sing, thyself is done.


