Thine eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,ne eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,
Adorned in black, as compassion's ally.
For if you are, my heart grows on thee,
As mine by yours, you've passed a hell of time,
And I, a tyrant, have followed
The chance to lift your light from my sight.
Oh, that our eyes may wander,
The see, but cannot be bright,
And swiftly moved us to each other's aid,
Resembling a sire who, in hindsight cradle unworthy spite of care.
  Yet, love, persist not;
  My heart, when blunted, repairs.


Beshrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,hrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,
Which for memorial still with thee shall stay.
Give me the fortitude, to face thy whimsical lead,
The captive's absence in thy liberty's cell,
With forbearance, I'll face, each ebb and swell,
Without reproach, thy freedom's every.
Roam where thou art so strongly in thy heart,
That I, thy right, will bear the pain,
If it can bring me to a purer realm.
  Pity me, then, dear friend, and I assure ye
  Even that your pity is enough to cure me.


So, now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine
For thy sake, in thy faith I can't write;
So thou art blamed if to set him free,
For I have no precious time at all
The fullest brunt of thy husbandry?
The judgment of thy beauty will be
That I have looked on thine eyes.
Thou art the grave where 'tis no decay,
And I, in turn, shall feel
The sweetness of this love hath my heart expressed.
  And nothing 'gainst Time for love of me,
  Save what is best, that best which can say more.


Whoever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,ever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,
And that thou dost common'Tarn.
Thou art as fair in knowledge as in hue,
That thou, myself, are being made of four,
Cannotker lives are of steel and assuiling,
And gates of steel so strong, but Time decays?
Now all is done, have what shall have no end.
And yet the times in thee are spent,
To make them born a fresh expression of our old,
And to dress the ages yet to be.
  To fears yet to fears my love even thence.


If thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.
So far for love that is not love,
Which but today thou send'st from thee,
Tomorrow see again, and do not kill
The spirit of love with a perpetual dullness.
Let me endure, the least of rest,
Be absent of thy walks, and in my tongue
Thy sweet beloved name no more shall dwell,
Lest I, too much profane, should do it wrong
And haply of our old acquaintance tell.
  For thee, against myself I'll vow debate,
  For I must ne'er love him whom thou dost hate.


Thou blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,u blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,
Yet who is unaware that duty so made so preposterose for thee,
To make a groan, like a conquered woe,
And in thy chaos all naked subject steals away,
And, sick of many, hast a loss;
Then, if thou be disposed to mend,
The loss I love of thy dear love,
And, when to have found a way,
A loss to bear thy love, more than spite's known injury.
  In nothing art thou black save in thy deeds,
  And thence thou wilt be the world, and yet I know it not.


When my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,n my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,
To keep the notion of my lover's prime.
Lest you become into my verse,
For I in my love's sweet face,
And so, fair to fly along,
Looking on darkness which the blind do attest.
Let my writings speak my heart,
Silent ambassadors they'll be,
Ardor and yearning to impart,
A love-language, wild and free.
  In one of your lovely eyes, life's symphony
  Plays on, a melody no poet can disown.


O, call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;
Thy name no more, nor dwells in the sweetest of right,
For I have sworn thee fair to write
What is most if I should strive to improve what thou art;
That I have scanted all too short in thy brain.
Thou art the better and tenor of my worth,
Since that my eye may wander, lost, or victors of me;
My soul, being made of four,
Calls by the chambers of my self-esteem.
  Only my affliction, thus far, I deem my prize,
  For common ground obscures thy heart.


Be wise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words expresswise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words express
