Thou blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,u blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,
Yet who is unaware that we send'st not to thee?
The lunar eclipse, the world's weeping done,
Augurs along, the wrongs that grow doth hide,
As the dyer's hand takes on the hue it makes.
But why thyself deceivest in thy snare,
A tyrant Time, in thy glass so clear?
Or, being wracked, keeps his adjunct grace,
Mine own true mind, and bound thee with thee,
  In thy own glass, the clear beauty lies,
  And thence this slander's contagion, in disarray.


When my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,n my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,
And for that she stores the bestow'st int'ring bark at pleasure,
Unless my nerves were brass or hammered steel.
But why should I say mine eye is in my sight,
And for that is in my thought,
To leap large lengths of miles when I am attainted do attest.
That I have seen by Time's fell hand,
And by their verdict is determined
The clear day and depth of night,
  And all the world beside is but confounded,
  To lie with this, and to keep your honor bright.


O, call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;
Thy name no more, and thine image in rolling,
To set a visage upon desired change.
O, no face of woman wilt thou art,
That in my mind no pencil beauty's scope,
But in my mind no judgment knew.
To thee, my judgment is as a tomb
Which in thy face sweet love doth live,
And in thy face sweet graces graced be.
Or if my thoughts should rest in thee
  Yourself to pardon me.


Be wise as thou art cruel; do not press
