Thine eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,ne eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,
In the turmoil of falsehood, where beauty's face is stained.
Yet, like the dyer's hand, I sometime close,
Indeed, the lowly touch of my heart's true wish,
As every alien pen, by cool well water,
Their hearts serve, and touch my heart's deep well.
So too, the lines of life, by time outlined,
Or by my hand in verse, can never be
As true to thee as when thou dost review
The clear-orbed where thou art.
  Thus, I'll stand mute, at times,
