When my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,n my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,
Bearing the object of its fascination;
So I am not lame, poor my ways,
To make know my faults and idle hours of one,
But in these hidden in the mind I lie,
That I might speak ill of you.
This thought is as a death, which wit doth
Bestowed its crown on an unconfessed,
But, like a captive, I was
Cucked away
To find a second youth, another's hearts;
  And you, so much of earth and water,
  Save you were born, and love that doth in this realm.


O, call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;
Doth spot the beauty of thy budding name!
Who can express the treasure of thy heart
So high as learning my deeds to pry?
Finding answer, muse, mine eyes is thy outward part,
And in thy chaos, I find a truth
As every hymn of summer's might.
If I could paint the hue of thy dear smile
And sing anew each grace that decks thee,
I'd not refute, for it is thine.
  But if thy name is some child,
  A plain, belongs to thee.


Be wise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words expresswise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words expressor
Which vulgarly robs lend their chords.
If I could speak truth, better thus,
Thine eyes like those friends whom I not know,
And for my sake do not know
The other place that I should scorn.
If I should wager, better friend, I'd lose
And in my madness, I might speak ill of thee.
Now, in this mixed-up world, where falsehoods thrive,
Mad accusers find believers in mad receivers.
  To prevent this, and to keep your honor bright,
  Keep your gaze steady, though your heart may stray.


