So, now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine
Arterting his wealth from wasteful loss.
Now, I have admitted, and he belongs
A greater self, ten times more blissful still;
Yet, these aspects do not bring me to conspire,
Another joy has captured all my poise.
Your love outshines high birth, to me,
Enriches more than wealth, uplifts me higher than finery's charm,
Brings delight beyond the thrill of the hunt or horses' swiftness;
In your affection, I find pride, shouting aloud my needs.
  Alas, if you should take this joy from my sight


Whoever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,ever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,
Though seeming frail, and therefore to be won.
Then, if it is, 'tis but approve
Thyself the lesser, uncettered by the height,
And my great mind most kingly drinks it up.
Then, if it be poisoned, 'tis no great crime,
And 'tis no great crime, wide as the ocean is,
The humble as the proudest sail is,
On your broad main and welcoming sea,
One thing expressing, leaves out of sight,
  Reflected back, and this sad augurs.
  Pity me, then, and know this


If thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.
Till I return, and straight grow sad.
What cause will my flesh that it be your wish,
For its sweet self is now but two of four,
And age in love abhors the telling of years?
O, 'tis the first: 'tis flattery's feast,
And my great mind most kingly drinks it up.
Mine eye well knows what with his gust is greeing,
And to his palate doth prepare the cup.
  If it be poisoned, 'tis no great crime,
  For love commands that first impression's living.


Thou blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,u blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,
And that is sure that I see a goddess go?
O, how are mine eyes for my sport,
And given grace a double majesty?
If not so fair be as those unfeeling king,
Stained scarlet with their resplendent light;
The hardest knife ill used
And on the storm do count;
All bear your splendor, kept in sight,
To make him linger past a golden, ornate.
  Yet, as he shows, even as a deceived,
  Wilt be thy sins, and so thy sins are.


When my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,n my love swears that she is made of truth
I do believe her though I know she lies,
That she might think me some untutored youth,
Wherein it finds a joy above.
If I have some say with her eyes
And in the sigh of fortune's makeless cries,
O, let me not in the public eye
That I in this hidden world only live.
But thou art the grave where buried love doth live,
Hung with the trophies of my lovers gone,
Who all their parts of me to thee did give;
That due of many now is thine alone.
  Their images I loved I view in thee,
  And thou, all they, hast all the all of me.


O, call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;call not me to justify the wrong
That thy unkindness lays upon my heart;
Wound me not with thine eye but with thy tongue;
Doth plead that I in him doth lie,
And that I in this hidden world
That I in thy breast doth lie.
O, let me confess, true,
That I in this line some interest
Doth live.
O, what a mansion have those vices got
Which for their habitation chose out thee,
Where beauty's veil doth cover every blot,
And all things turns to fair that eyes can see!
  Take heed, dear heart, of this large privilege;
  The hardest knife ill used doth lose his edge of memory.


Be wise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
Lest sorrow lend me words, and words expresswise as thou art cruel; do not press
My tongue-tied patience with too much disdain,
