Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,ne eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,
And tearing my dearest friend as a glove.
So I've kept them not in chests or strongboxes bound,
But in the heart's vast chamber, where dreams were born and stored,
By the grave's imprint, I've been.
In quietude, I ponder thee,
The beauty that your heart may serve,
Indeed, I've found its faults, and so,
Within your sternest gaze, I now surrender,
But do not let your ire obscure your cheer.
  My heart insists that love may dwell,
  And in that love, I find sweet solace.


