Thine eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,ne eyes I love, and they, as pitying me,
Knowing thy heart torment me with disdain,
Have put on black, and loving mourners be,
In sympathy, tearing my heart from sight.
Thou art the world's art,
And in the darkness, see the skies,
Adorned with their darkened robe,
A mother's gentle verse, wasting pride,
And in her quiet lair,
Lest her lonely wolf then did sing.
No need for ablest wreath of such grand,
As I, perhaps, did give
Your beauty to thy name.
  In nothing art thou black save in thy deeds,
  And thence this slander as I think proceeds.


Beshrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,hrew that heart that makes my heart to groan
For that deep wound it gives my friend and me.
Is 't not enough to torture me alone,
And bind my dearest friend to suffer, see?
It is the case where misers find
In my own circumstance, the sovereign's bane doth stay.
My eye, well knowing, readies the deceit,
A venial error, should love lead defeat.
For I have sworn thee fair, and bound my fate,
To one whose heart is as black as pitch,
And to thy fair flower add something more;
  But if thy unworthiness hath wrought this plight,
  More worthy I to share thy night.


So, now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine now I have confessed that he is thine
And I myself am mortgaged to thy will,
Myself I'll forfeit, so that other mine
Arterting his wealth, and is partly blind.
Seems the heart's high courtant grace,
That in his prime is hanging still,
And hath his wealth from my sight,
Making his own worth a limit past my own heart.
Now, all is done, have what shall have no end.
Mine appetite I never more will grind
On newer proof, to try an older friend,
A god in love, to whom I am confined.
  Then give me welcome, next my heaven the best,
  Even to thy pure and most most loving breast.


Whoever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,ever hath her wish, thou hast thy will,
And will to boot, and will in overplus.
More than enough am I that vex thee still,
Though seeming frail and do not know thy time.
Thou canst not, love, to die well,
As she hath her exister might,
And I, in turn, believe her,
To make him groan,
And in her woe, the aged.
But if thou wilt, my love is fresh,
And age's rich bounty shall not be so stout,
Nor he that wide world and all her transient pride.
  For thou art so possessed with murd'rous hate
  That I will be a king, and all it represents.


If thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.thy soul check thee that I come so near,
Swear to thy blind soul that I was thy will,
And will, thy soul knows, is admitted there.
Till I return, of posting is no need.
O, what a modern quill doth flattery find
When it hath not the show of thy worth?
Then, soul, be of thyself the world,
Be of thyself thy worth, and I am still weep.
Then, if he thrive and I be cast off,
For love's proud legacy lives,
If it be thyself to bear.
  Or, if my corporeal self were to thy aid,
  The worst was this: my love was my decay.


Thou blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,u blind fool, Love, what dost thou to mine eyes
That they behold and see not what they see?
They know what beauty is, see where it lies,
Thy face, the truth, that so many have enrolled.
Yet, Love, you've kept so fair,
As the blind eye becomes the heart's company,
In love's own power, where hearts are swerving.
Why should my heart believe in twisted lines,
When it knows well the unity of our song?
Or should my eyes, which gaze, insist on division,
To crown the lie with beauty all along?
  In truth and right, my heart and eyes have wandered,
  And now to illusion's snare they've tethered.


